            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we found some substantial air leakage in the following locations:



** The old chimney location for the previous furnace does not appear to be sealed and is allowing significant heat loss from your house.



** The wood stove is drawing it's combustion air from inside the house and, although the wood stove door is closed, there is still interior heated air going up the woodstove chimney - even when the woodstove iis not being used.



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, as well as the weatherstripping around the exterior door is allowing air leakage &amp; heat loss. There is also air leakage along the baseboard of the exterior walls.



Savings: For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially save $ 139 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.0 GJ, and qualify for a $500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 3.5 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. There is a section of wall (29% of the total main floor perimeter) that you are planning to renovate, removing the batt insulation &amp; installing a minimum R22 spray ployurethane foam insulation. You had also mentioned a longer term plan to perhaps renovate the exterior of 100% of the house to add another R15 to the exterior walls.



I have modeled both of these options for you. 



Rebate:

By increasing 29% of your walls by a minimum R10, you could potentially save $ 85 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 870 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), If you later decided to add R15 to 100% of the exterior of the main floor walls (and the DHERP is still in place), you could potentially save another $ 720 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy use by another 20.9 GJ, and qualify for another $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <windows>

You are currently planning to replace 4 older triple pane windows in your home with new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full-frame replacement triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Only windows being replaced in conjunction with an eligible Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) wall insulation upgrade (and within the section of walls being upgraded) are eligible for a rebate, 3 of the 4 windows you are planning to upgrade could therefore be eligible for a window rebate.



You had also mentioned a longer term plan to perhaps renovate the exterior of 100% of the house to add another R15 to the exterior walls. If you were to do this  (and the DHERP is still in place), you could be eligible to replace other older windows on the main floor of your house. 



Rebates:

By replacing the 4 windows with full-frame, triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially  save $ 127 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ and qualify for $ 250 per window (for the 3 eligible window totaling $ 750 in rebates). Please review the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program Guildelines for the minimum specifications required for windows under the program. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Should you consider a switch to a propane furnace, this would represent an opportunity for you to consider replacing this electric tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher (I modeled one that is 0.95).



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save $ 1,022 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your NTI Caprice oil boiler is a direct vent model that brings in its' own combustion air from outside and also sidewall vents the combustion gases, and the brochure indicates an 86% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). The oil boiler replaced a previous oil furnace, so now the current oil boiler incorporates a fancoil to circulate warm air throughout the house.



This oil boiler was set up to also heat your domestic hot water, however you had mentioned the water was never hot enough so you now strictly use the hot water tank in "all electric" mode. Perhaps you may want to consider a switch to propane and look at higher efficiency options for both your heating &amp; hot water.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Savings:

There is a bonus rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) for installing eligible heating equipment, in conjunction with an eligible wall insulation upgrade. By replacing your current oil boiler with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,075 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (double the standard rebate the AEA provides)  from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 3kitchen range hood fan, bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.4 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler is direct vented to the exterior of the house, however the wood stove draws its' combustion air from inside the house and is susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Wood Stove Combustion Air



The woodstove does not have a dedicated outside combustiion air supply directly sealed to the stove, so it therefore draws inside air to burn and is susceptible to possible backdraft. In addition, the woodstove will draw some air from inside the house up through the chimney at all times, even when the woodstove is not operating &amp; the door is shut.



I have estimated the cost of this annual heat loss from your home as $ 76.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.70 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Nick Walker, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Nick.Walker@aea.nt.ca



